ARM: do not symbolise addresses when printing an Oops

The vendor xm_*.ko modules make module symbol lookup fault (get_ksymbol
dereferences a NULL strtab). Because that lookup is reached from the Oops
printer itself, the first fault in any vendor module becomes an endless
recursive fault storm: no usable trace and the board wedges until power
is cycled. For a camera that is unacceptable - a driver fault in a
teardown path must kill the process, not the device. Printing raw
addresses breaks the recursion; module addresses are still resolvable
offline against /sys/module/<mod>/sections/.text.

--- a/arch/arm/kernel/traps.c	2026-07-26 10:28:20.562434568 +0000
+++ b/arch/arm/kernel/traps.c	2026-07-26 10:28:20.602435033 +0000
@@ -64,7 +64,14 @@
 
 void dump_backtrace_entry(unsigned long where, unsigned long from, unsigned long frame)
 {
-#ifdef CONFIG_KALLSYMS
+/*
+ * Deliberately NOT symbolising here: on this platform resolving a module
+ * address can fault inside get_ksymbol() (NULL strtab), and doing that from
+ * within the Oops printer turns the first fault into an unrecoverable
+ * recursive fault storm that wedges the board. Raw addresses keep an Oops
+ * an ordinary Oops: the faulting task dies, the system survives.
+ */
+#if 0
 	printk("[<%08lx>] (%ps) from [<%08lx>] (%pS)\n", where, (void *)where, from, (void *)from);
 #else
 	printk("Function entered at [<%08lx>] from [<%08lx>]\n", where, from);
--- a/arch/arm/kernel/process.c	2026-07-26 10:28:20.572434684 +0000
+++ b/arch/arm/kernel/process.c	2026-07-26 10:28:20.602435033 +0000
@@ -118,8 +118,9 @@
 
 	show_regs_print_info(KERN_DEFAULT);
 
-	print_symbol("PC is at %s\n", instruction_pointer(regs));
-	print_symbol("LR is at %s\n", regs->ARM_lr);
+	/* raw addresses only - see the note in dump_backtrace_entry() */
+	printk("PC is at 0x%08lx\n", instruction_pointer(regs));
+	printk("LR is at 0x%08lx\n", (unsigned long)regs->ARM_lr);
 	printk("pc : [<%08lx>]    lr : [<%08lx>]    psr: %08lx\n"
 	       "sp : %08lx  ip : %08lx  fp : %08lx\n",
 		regs->ARM_pc, regs->ARM_lr, regs->ARM_cpsr,
